r need\\n137:    to look outside the packet blocks acceptance. Record the defect; do not\\n138:    silently repair it while assigning PASS. Return substantive/structural defects to authoring and review the frozen\\n139:    revision; eligible background notes need no author rewrite. Routine completion does not require a\\n140:    separate user interruption for every omission. Distinguish a representation\\n141:    defect from a substantive mathematical gap under the existing gap policy.\\n142: \\n143: The code rejects explicit malformed references, inventories, scopes, guards\\n144: and receipts. It does not infer hidden hypotheses from property words in types,\\n145: or decide theorem applications by matching a list of mathematical names.\\n146: These semantic obligations apply equally to every subject and remain part of\\n147: author inspection and Audit A. An omitted inventory entry or unnamed\\n148: theorem hidden in prose still requires actual semantic review. Freshness and\\n149: truthful reviewer identity need execution evidence; record shape/hashes cannot\\n150: authenticate them. No natural-language audit is a Lean kernel certificate.\"}"}}
{"i":4,"result":{"status":"fulfilled","value":"{\"path\": \"nl0/references/external-theorems.md\", \"total_lines\": 128, \"start\": 1, \"end\": 128, \"start_column\": 0, \"end_column\": 76, \"requested_end\": 250, \"has_more\": false, \"next_start\": null, \"next_column\": 0, \"next_end\": null, \"text\": \"1: # External-theorem review: required Audit A standard\\n2: \\n3: Apply this standard to every retained E and every actual use of E in an R.\\n4: An E keeps the full source theorem; a derived specialization or substantive\\n5: translation is an internal N. Use the data contract's audit version 2. These\\n6: are reviewer obligations: a filled record or matching hash cannot establish\\n7: that a publication or a mathematical assertion is genuine.\\n8: \\n9: ## 1. Document identity\\n10: \\n11: Obtain and read the actual primary document. Match author, title, source kind,\\n12: publication/release information and exact edition/version to its provenance.\\n13: Record a traceable original source, retrieval date, local document and hash.\\n14: A search snippet, model answer, secondary citation, or functioning DOI/URL\\n15: alone does not establish identity. Books, papers and reliable preprints can\\n16: qualify; a DOI and journal publication are not universal requirements. Record\\n17: the source category and grounds for treating it as an admissible source.\\n18: \\n19: For missing originals, follow [ordered source retrieval](source-retrieval.md):\\n20: arXiv, Google full text, Google DOI discovery and confirmed-DOI Sci-Hub lookup.\\n21: Use normal browser readback when needed; unresolved access and verification\\n22: failures remain recorded. Then use the [conditional-source\\n23: procedure](conditional-sources.md) for unavailable text. Unavailable checks remain UNVERIFIED. A complete explicit\\n24: secondary statement can support a disclosed conditional run, not an ordinary\\n25: source-verification PASS.\\n26: \\n27: ## 2. Occurrence of the result\\n28: \\n29: Locate the claimed result in that document and version. Record theorem,\\n30: lemma, proposition or corollary label and page/section/paragraph/equation as\\n31: available; unnumbered results require an equally reproducible locator. Retain\\n32: the relevant primary text or a precise readback record bound to the document.\\n33: If the cited document merely points elsewhere without actually stating the\\n34: claimed result, follow and correct the source. An explicitly stated theorem in\\n35: an authoritative exposition can be cited as such; record its proof attribution\\n36: without claiming to have audited the referenced proof recursively.\\n37: Distinguish proved assertions from conjectures, questions, heuristics and\\n38: numerical observations. Preserve every condition of a conditional result.\\n39: \\n40: ## 3. Statement fidelity\\n41: \\n42: Compare the original full statement with the E statement, explicitly covering\\n43: objects, domains, scalar fields, spaces, hypotheses, regularity, quantifier\\n44: order, parameter intervals/endpoints, degeneracies, constant dependencies and\\n45: the exact conclusion (including a.e./everywhere, topology, uniformity,\\n46: existence/uniqueness and local/global scope). Record the notation map.\\n47: Faithful mathematical meaning, not word-for-word copying, is required.\\n48: Alpha-renaming is allowed. Never turn forall-x-exists-C_x into exists-C-forall-x.\\n49: \\n50: ## 4. Complete context and definitions\\n51: \\n52: Read the surrounding standing assumptions, definitions and conventions that\\n53: govern the result, including referenced context needed to understand it.\\n54: Capture any omitted chapter-wide hypotheses and normalization of measures,\\n55: norms, transforms, signs or scales. Map the source objects to the global D\\n56: ledger. Do not hide a condition outside the proof graph. A substantive\\n57: conversion requires an N proof; a renamed symbol is not that proof.\\n58: \\n59: ## 5. Version status and source eligibility\\n60: \\n61: Match every locator and statement to the chosen version. Check available\\n62: primary version histories, corrections, errata or withdrawal information at\\n63: the recorded date and scope. Record relevant findings and their effect;\\n64: explain use of an older version. Absence of a discovered correction is not\\n65: a guarantee that none exists. Unresolved conflicts remain UNVERIFIED.\\n66: Never import the target, an unproved project lemma, or a circular dependency\\n67: as E. Previously proved project modules require an expanded graph or a\\n68: versioned proof handoff. Do not attribute a combination of sources and a\\n69: project bridge to a single published theorem. A conjecture-dependent theorem\\n70: must retain that condition, which must be supplied legitimately at application.\\n71: \\n72: ## 6. Every actual application\\n73: \\n74: Give each actual E application an inference-local A ID. The same E applied\\n75: twice with different bindings has two A records, even within the same R.\\n76: For each A inspect the parameter/object bindings, prove every source\\n77: hypothesis from declared inputs or displayed local derivations, check types,\\n78: definitions and parameter ranges, identify substantive bridge N nodes, and\\n79: verify the resulting conclusion supports R's output. Record the evidence for\\n80: each individual hypothesis. “The theorem applies” is not a mapping.\\n81: Do not add an unproved hypothesis to close an application. An E passed at its\\n82: source does not automatically pass at any application site.\\n83: \\n84: ## 7. Ownership of derived work\\n85: \\n86: Put substantive specialization, scaling, change of normalization/domain/space/\\n87: norm/representation, combinations of sources, and prerequisite lemmas in N.\\n88: Each N lists its actual E/M/H/earlier-N inputs and its argument. Do not enlarge\\n89: the quoted theorem. Explicit elementary calculations and logical instantiation\\n90: may remain inside R; justify them rather than imposing needless graph nodes.\\n91: \\n92: ## 8. Separate decisions and reproducible evidence\\n93: \\n94: For each E write five separate review records: `literature_identity`,\\n95: `result_location`, `statement_fidelity`, `context_completeness`, and\\n96: `version_and_eligibility`. Each contains verdict, concrete notes and hashed\\n97: evidence. The first three must never be merged into one blanket PASS.\\n98: For each R/A write separate binding, per-hypothesis and conclusion reviews,\\n99: the source ID and the proved bridge IDs. Record actual reviewer, carrier,\\n100: shared context and reference material read. Self-audit is not independent.\\n101: \\n102: Use PASS only after the check was performed with adequate evidence; FAIL for\\n103: an established mismatch/omission/invalid application; UNVERIFIED when necessary\\n104: evidence is missing. Inaccessible text does not establish nonexistence. An\\n105: established absence of the claimed result in the cited source is a FAIL.\\n106: Retain failed records and the exact reason, source location and affected nodes.\\n107: \\n108: ## 9. Acceptance, invalidation and trust boundary\\n109: \\n110: Every retained E check and every application must PASS before the graph can\\n111: receive DRAFT-AUDIT-PASSED. A load-bearing FAIL blocks acceptance. Source-verification UNVERIFIED can\\n112: support only the explicitly qualified continuation above; other unresolved\\n113: mathematical checks still block it. Ordinary whole-proof acceptance requires\\n114: every retained check to pass. Never replace a\\n115: missing verification with a historical audit or a Lean build. Changes to the\\n116: source/version, statement, definitions, bindings or condition proofs invalidate\\n117: affected reviews; bind the replacement whole receipt to new canonical hashes.\\n118: \\n119: An inaccessible source suspends its citation verification, not the rest of\\n120: the workflow: continue available checks and explicitly conditional reasoning,\\n121: then report the unfinished citation checks and the UNVERIFIED overall audit\\n122: outcome. Descendants can be proved only relative to the explicit E assumption; the\\n123: whole result cannot receive an unconditional pass based on that source, and source inaccessibility alone is not a finding of mathematical error.\\n124: \\n125: List all external dependencies in the final verdict. This audit is relative\\n126: to those identified theorems; it does not independently reprove their published\\n127: proofs or establish a Lean proof of E. Mechanical checks establish record\\n128: coverage and byte consistency, not authenticity or mathematical correctness.\"}"}}
